'''''Echinocereus reichenbachii''''' (commonly known as '''lace''' or '''hedgehog''' cactus) is a [[perennial plant]] and [[shrub]] in the [[cactus]] family. The species is [[native plant|native]] to the [[Chihuahuan Desert]] and parts of northern Mexico and the southern United States, where they grow at elevations up to {{convert|1500|meter}}. This cactus earned the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].


==Description==
[[File:Lace Cactus (Echinocereus reichenbachii) 3.jpg|thumb|left|alt=A color picture of a green and cactus with white spines|''E. reichenbachii'']]
''Echinocereus reichenbachii'' is a [[perennial plant]] and [[shrub]]. It is one of the smaller ''Echinocereus'' species.{{sfn|Powell|Weedin|2004|p=245}}  Immature specimens are spherical, and as they grow they become cylindrical.{{sfn|Ladybird Johnson Wildflower Center}} Plants are solitary or multi-branched in clusters of as many as twelve, with erect stems with 10 to 19 slightly undulate ribs. They reach {{convert|7.5 to 30|cm}} tall and {{convert|4 to 10|cm}} wide.{{sfn|Flora of North America}} The stems are dark green and obscured by the [[Thorns, spines, and prickles|spines]], especially when the plant is dehydrated. [[Areole]]s are elliptical or oval.{{sfn|Powell|Weedin|2004|p=245}} They are spaced {{convert|1 to 6|mm}} apart, with 15 to 36 [[Thorns, spines, and prickles|spines]] each. The spines are tan, to brown, black, purplish black, or pink, and the tips are usually darker than the shaft. The central spines tend to be the darkest. There are up to 7 central spines per areole; they are {{convert| 1 to 6|mm}} long.{{sfn|Flora of North America}}

Plants flower in early May and late June, and they fruit 6 to 10 weeks after flowering.{{sfn|Flora of North America}} Flowers open for just one day, but [[anthesis]] is usually staggered so plants have blooming flowers for a full week; buds are covered in white wool that hides the fruit as it develops.{{sfn|Powell|Weedin|2004|p=245}} The purple or pink flowers bloom in early May to late June, growing to approximately {{convert|4.5 to 8|cm}} by {{convert|5 to 10|cm}}. The flower's inner [[tepal]]s are silvery pink or magenta; the outer portions are white, crimson, green, or multicolored. They are approximately {{convert|4.5 to 8|cm}} by {{convert|5 to 10|cm}}, and the flower tubes are {{convert|22 to 40|mm}} by {{convert|10 to 30|mm}}. The tube hairs are {{convert|5 to 15|mm}} long, and the nectar chamber is {{convert|2 to 5|mm}} deep.{{sfn|Flora of North America}} Flowers have 30 to 50 petals each, which are ragged or notched. [[Pistil]]s are multi-lobed and green, and [[stamen]]s are cream-colored or yellow.{{sfn|Ladybird Johnson Wildflower Center}} Fruits are various shades of green and {{convert|15 to 28|mm}} long.{{sfn|Flora of North America}}

